<tr bgcolor=#F5DEB3><td colspan=2 align=center valign=middle>The Curious Bird</td></tr>
<tr bgcolor=#F5DEB3><td colspan=2 align=left valign=middle>Magpye slipped quietly along the rockface, edging closer and closer to the garden. Behind her, the other children peered around the corner, whispering excitedly to each other.
<br><br>
"Betcha she doesn't do it," one child whispered.
<br><br>
"Betcha she does!" retorted another.
<br><br>
A third frantically hushed the rest, not wanting to attract the fun-spoiling attention of the adults labouring nearby.
<br><br>
Magpye slowly eased herself up the fence, then after a quick glance towards the adults gathered near the house, peered into the field.
<br><br>
Shakey was there, just a few feet beyond the fence. He was stumping from side to hide, clutching at his big pumpkin-shaped head. She edged closer, intending to scale the fence, then jumped back as Shakey screamed with maniacal laughter, her heart pounding in her chest.
<br><br>
At the sound, the other children finally fell silent for a moment.
<br><br>
"Magpye," hissed the ringleader, "come back, it's too dangerous."
<br><br>
"No," she hissed in return, jaw setting stubbornly as she lowered her head pugnaciously, "I said I'd do it, and I'm going to do it!"
<br><br>
Before she could get frightened and change her own mind, she whirled and clambered over the fence, and scurried towards the ominously tall scarecrow. Coming to an abrupt stop just behind him, she swallowed nervously, screwing up her courage. She wasn't scared of an old scarecrow like Shakey! Why, half the hay was already missing from his head...
<br><br>
Targetting a bit of straw that hung out the back of his coat, she snatched it free and then spun to run back to the fence...and smacked right into Reebo Leafsway. Catching her wrist, he plucked the bit of straw from her hand, and shook it at her, the wispy tip near hitting her nose.
<br><br>
"Magpye, what are you doing with this," he asked. "You know children aren't allowed in the garden, and especially, children are supposed to avoid getting too close to Shakey. He's getting...difficult...in his old age."
<br><br>
"I'm sorry, Reebo," she answered, hanging her head and scuffing at the freshly turned soil with one bare foot. "I didn't mean to do anything bad. It's just...just..."
<br><br>
"Just that someone dared you to," he finished her sentence. "Tell me, if someone dared you to jump in the river and tease the pirhana, would you do that too?"
<br><br>
At her striken silence, he burst into laughter. "By the look on your face, you've done just that. I can't decide if you're brave...or just foolhardy."
<br><br>
Smiling broadly, he changed his grip from her wrist to her shoulder. "Come along, little bird," he ordered, smiling, "I think I need to have a talk with your father."
<br><br>
<HR>
Magpye's father shook his head sadlly. "I just don't know what to do with her, Reebo," he said. "From birth, I expected her to follow in my footsteps and be a good respectable Rogue like myself. That's why my wife and I named her Magpye, after the bird that steals shiney objects."
<br><br>
"But she's far to honest for her own good, and I'm constantly hearing of her getting into one scrape or another. Swimming in the river, sneaking out of the city to go steal honeycomb from the Bixies...why, I even heard from someone that she'd been seen giving that no-good brownie Nillipuss a piece of her mind after he stole a bit of jum-jum stalk from one of her playmates. I suppose it's the lack of a mother that's done it...I don't have the time to look after her properly by myself." He looked shame-faced.
<br><br>
"Hmmm...sounds like she's quite strong-willed all right," Leebo answered. "Tell me...would you have any objections to my having her work around the farm for a while? I want to see just what she's like for myself...I've heard a surprising lot about her, given she's such a young lass."
<br><br>
Magpye's father glanced around to make sure she wasn't in earshot, then leaned close to Rebbo. "You think she might go for a druid, don't you," he asked, quietly.
<br><br>
Reebo was silent a moment, then gave a tiny nod.
<br><br>
"She might, if the stories I've heard have a grain of truth. On the surface, she's terribly reckless...yet everything she's done has been well-planned and carefully executed. I suspect a brain lurks behind those guiless green eyes. If she's got the knack, as well...." he trailed off, speculatively.
<br><br>
<HR>
Magpye leaned on her shovel for a moment, pushy her sweaty hair out of her eyes with the back on one dusty hand. She'd finished turning the field, and tomorrow she'd be planting the new crop of carrots. Trudging over to the rock face, she dropped her shovel and sat down in the shade, enjoying the relative coolness after the heat of the afternoon sun.
<br><br>
Shakey stumped towards her, as he followed his overly meandering patrol route along the edge of the field. Every few steps he'd stop and clutch his head, then scream with laughter.
<br><br>
"Only you're not really laughing, are you," she muttered, watching him lurch past. You know something is wrong with you, but you don't know what it is, and even if you did, you couldn't tell us."
<br><br>
"That's rather observant of you," a shrill voice piped up by her side, making her jump in the air with surprise. Turning, she saw Nillipuss the Brownie standing there, calming chewing on a stalk of Jum-Jum. He smiled and winked, then placed one finger to his lips.
<br><br>
"Shush, I'm taking a break," he said. "Seems I can't throw a tantrum these days without some foulhardy soul trying to kill me for my little treasures."
<br><br>
Magpye giggled. She'd never seen the brownie not being annoying before.
<br><br>
"There's something wrong with Shakey, all right," the brownie continued. "I'm a long lived little guy, and I can remember him when he was brand new, with a full head of hay and a body well-stuffed with straw. Used to come and chat with him at night, for hours on end. He didn't have much conversation, mainly stuff about crop rotation and potatoe beetles and so on, but he was the friendliest soul I knew here."
<br><br>
"Then about, oh, fifty-odd years about, when Leebo Leafsway that was, the young Leafsway's grandfather, was head druid, they installed that forge over there...too close to the house. First dry night it got left with coals banked in it, the wall started smouldering."
<br><br>
"Well, if there's one thing a good scarecrow knows, it's that fires are bad. Old Shakey tried to raise the alarm, and when that failed, he ran down to the river, got himself good and sopping wet, then ran back and used handfuls of his own wet straw to beat out the flames. Poor fellow hasn't been the same since...his head is full of frizzled bits and mildew now."
<br><br>
"Oh, that's terrible!" Magpye exclaimed. "Can't he be fixed?"
<br><br>
"Sure, sure he can," the brownie stated, swallowing the lat of his Jum-Jum stalk. "If someone can discover how to make the right sort of hay to restuff his head with. The scarecrows were only a brief fad, people stopped getting them made after a bunch of them ran wild in the Karanas. Nobody remembers any more about who made them or what ingredients they used. Bye now. And remember...you didn't see me, you didn't speak to me, and I wasn't nice."
<br><br>
Winking, the brownie turned and ran off into the shadows.
<br><br>
Magpye eyed Shakey speculatively.
<br><br>
"Someday, Shakey, I'm going to restuff your head," she promised.
<br><br>
<HR>
Magpye was never quite sure just exactly when she made the decision to become a druid. At first she'd just worked in the fields and helped out in the store. When she turned twelve, Daleen showed her how to paint her toenails and pumice her heels, and she left home and moved in with the other apprentices in the cave across the river. Within the year, Leebo had arranged for her graduation ceremony from apprentice sage to actual druid.
<br><br>
Her nerves were shot that day. She'd been told to make herself scare while preperations were underway, so she wandered out to the fields and talked to Shakey for a while, and even hunted Nillipuss down where he was basking in the sun on a high ledge, and talked with him for a while too. She spent the afternoon perched on a rock near the bank, watching travellors come and go. So many different people passed through her little city. Some of them were quite frighteningly tall, while others were comfortably short. Most fell somewhere in the middle.
<br><br>
As the long day was slowly dragging to a close, an elderly wood elf hobbled over and sat on the rock near her, sighing with relief as he got off his feet.
<br><br>
"Greeting, young halfling," he said, in beautifully correct Halfling. "It seems that every time 've passed here this afternoon, you've been sitting here, watching people pass. Have you nothing you should be doing?"
<br><br>
She blushed. "I've been told to stay out of the way, today," she answered, "I'm graduating tonight and they don't want me getting underfoot while they prepare things."
<br><br>
The old elf grinned, "Ah, into what occupation?" he asked, curious.
<br><br>
"I'm going to be a druid, " she answered.
<br><br>
"Really?" he asked, "And how did you come to pick that particular field?"
<br><br>
And so she spent several hours happily chatting with the old elf, telling him about her childhood, and the escapades she used to pull off, and her apprenticeship since.
<br><br>
<HR>
The ceremony that night was a wonderfully solemn occasion. Attended by a select handful of her fellow apprentices, and by her father, beaming as if he would burst with pride, she was sworn in as a follower of Karana, and pledged to guard nature from all that was unnatural, and from those who would exploit it for their own gain.
<br><br>
Her father had a special gift for her, that he presented to her at the end of the ceremony - a complete set of leather armor, to help protect her from the dangers she would be facing in her new life.
<br><br>
Then Leebo gestured to someone at the back of the room. A tall (comparitively speaking) figure stood up, and advanced to the front. It was the elderly Wood Elf that Magpye had spent the afternoon chatting with.
<br><br>
"Young Magpye," he said, "I too would like to give you a gift. I am retiring this evening, and have already given away much of my best weapons and armor...but I have a few trifles that might be of use to a young druid."
<br><br>
"Here is a staff for you to fight with. It is called a Jaggedpine Crook, and I have had it since I was a very young druid. You will acquire better weapons some day, but for now this is a good weapon for you to use."
<br><br>
"And this helm will also be of use to you. It is made of a special kind of turtle shell, and can only be found in Southern Karana."
<br><br>
"And finally, here are a few baubles to wear on your fingers and ears, that will help you along as well."
<br><br>
Surprised, and grateful, Magpye thanked the old elf profusely.
<br><br>
He smiled. "Wear them in good health, young Magpye," he said, "and be true to your calling." Turning, he left the room and dissapeared into the night, a flare of magelight the final sign of his departure.
<br><br>
<HR>
The following year was a joyous one for Magpye. She learned her skills quickly, fighting the orcs, goblins, and undead creatures that were a constant threat to her homeland. She also learned how to spot when a speciies of animal was getting overpopulated, and cull them accordingly. She made several new friends about town, as she worked at simple errands to raise the small amount of coinage necessary to keep herself fed, sheltered, and clothed.
<br><br>
One evening, at the beginning of her fifth season as a druid, she found herself sitting in the Fool's Gold, chatting with the barmaid and a dwarf who'd been renting a room there for some time.
<br><br>
"Straight-laced bunch of men you've got around here," the dwarf growled into his beer, "I can't stop for two minutes to water a tree without some Deputy know-it-all popping out of the woodwork and telling me to move along."
<br><br>
"Aye," agreed the barmaid, "seems the free spirits always leave town and the stick-in-the-mud types linger. I hear a lot of them head off to where you come from, Lindo...Butcherblock, or whatever it's called."
<br><br>
"Yes, a lot of them head that way," the dwarf agreed, "we've lots of small folk over on Faydwer, not as many of the bigguns as you have over here. Those barbarians...makes my blood crawl to think about how tall they are! Imagine how much food it must take to keep one of them properly fed...or how much ale it takes to get them drunk."
<br><br>
As he mournfully eyed his own empty glass, the barmaid laughed and topped it up. "It's really too bad," she said, "I'd love to travel and see the sights, some day...and I hear your drawves are fine craftspeople, too, with many lovely and unique things for people to buy."
<br><br>
The dwarf laughed. "Depends on what you're in the market for," he answered, "we're more given to arms and armor then to baubles, though we have a few of those as well."
<br><br>
"Our armorers are the best on Norrath though," he continued, "why, they can even make armor that's safe for a young druid like your friend here to wear...and much prettier then those rough leathers she's wearing now."
<br><br>
Magpye blushed, knowing her leathers were already showing some bad signs of heavy wear.
<br><br>
"Surely such armor would be out of my reach in price," she replied, "it must be a very special ore, for it not to disrupt the energies that we use in working with nature."
<br><br>
The dwarf laughed. "Not ore at all, little one, but the carapaces and legs of some particularly thick-shelled beetles that infest my homeland. Its not as strong as metal, so it's only used for beginning warriors...and is quite cheap, if you hunt down your own scarab pieces. A beautiful irridescent green it is, too."
<br><br>
Magpye's ears perked up at that. She desperatly wanted to replace her aging leathers...but preferred to skrimp and save to replace them with something better, rather then more of the same. She was about to grill the dwarf for more details about this scarab shell armor when a crowd of drunked halfling men came spilling down from the casino upstairs. They noisily crowded up to the bar, and she quickly found herself squeezed into a corner by herself.
<br><br>
By the time the barmaid had them all either served or out the door, the dwarf had dissapeared off to his quarters. The barmaid shock her hed in dissaproval at the chaos that the drunken halflings had caused.
<br><br>
"I swear," she exclaimed, "there's not a male halfling worth getting to know around here any more! If I wasn't so fond of this city, I'd quit my job and go travelling, an see the sights...and find me some nice lusty young thing with hairy toes. You know what they say, about how the hairier the feet are..."
<br><br>
She laughed bawdily, winked, then sashayed off to serve a loudly drunk halfling at the other end of the bar. Magpye waved halfheartedly after her, then returned to her own little flat.
<br><br>
<HR>
Her sleep that night was restless. She kept picturing herself, clad in rich green to match her eyes, seeing distant lands, meeting strange people...
<br><br>
"Go," a voice seemed to whisper in her head, "go and find your destiny."
<br><br>
The sun had not yet risen when she awoke the next morning. Hastily bundling her few belongings into a backpack, she scribbled an explanatory note to Reebo, and slipped it under the farmhouse door as she headed for the south gate, that led to Kithicor and the world beyond.
<br><br>
Somehow, she wasn't at all surprised to see Reebo already waiting there as she approached.
<br><br>
"I thought it was about time for you to be leaving us," he said, "you have a wandering spirit...and I seemed to feel Karana's voice in the night. My blessing on your travels, child...you are younger for this then I might have wished, but I would not see you wither on the vine for lack of proper care and nourishment."
<br><br>
Raising his hand in benediction, he invoked the powers of Karana, giving Magpye the speed to get her quickly past the dangers she would face in he travels. After dropping a final formal bow towards him, she passed through the gates, and raced eastwards through the mists of Kithicor, the sunlit slopes of the Butcherblock Mountains firmly pictured in her head.
